/* Beat 7. The tree, alive and consumed.
 *
 * FOUNDER IS AMBER AND MUTATED IS VIOLET, matching CreatureCard so one
 * creature does not change colour between the roster and the tree. The
 * previous pairing was a blue founder and a gold mutation, which collided
 * with the card's gold founder ring.
 *
 * THOSE TWO COLOURS ARE ALSO TWO SPECIES' COLOURS - Hollow is exactly
 * --violet and Skitter exactly --amber, both at dE 0.0, measured in Task 6.
 *
 * SETTLED IN TASK 13, AND NEITHER COLOUR MOVED. The six proxies were drawn,
 * tinted and captured, and the render moved the problem: on any screen that
 * DRAWS a creature the collision is mild, because a 2px founder frame and a
 * gold animal are different shapes in different places and bible 10.4's
 * "species colour is reinforced by silhouette" is doing its job. THIS screen
 * draws no creature at all, so Founder and Mutated were each carried by a
 * 3px border and nothing else - a 10.4 violation on its own terms, before
 * any species colour existed, and the reason the rails read as species
 * marks. The fix was a second channel, not a repaint: `.node__mark` below
 * writes all three facts in words. Moving the UI roles to new colours and
 * moving the two species colours were both costed and rejected -
 * implementation/results/species-collision.md has the evidence and the
 * reasoning. PaletteContrastTests' comment carries the same note from the
 * other end.
 *
 * WHAT LEFT THIS FILE IN PHASE 8 TASK 9: the root's gutter padding and the
 * `__title` rule are the scaffold's header and content region; the
 * `__next` margin is the CTA row's padding; `__notice` is gone entirely,
 * replaced by an `EmptyState` that exists only when the tree is empty rather
 * than a banner that drew an empty amber strip on every populated tree.
 */
.lineage-view { flex-grow: 1; }

.lineage-view__generations { flex-direction: column; }

/* A ROW OF CELLS AND A ROW OF CREATURES ARE BOTH THE CONTAINER'S JOB, and
 * StatCell.uss's closing note is why they are declared here rather than in
 * the components: a component's sheet reaches the component and its
 * descendants and never its parent, so nothing a StatCell carries can make
 * the strip holding it a row. The -4px cancels the half-gap a cell carries
 * on each of its own outer sides, so the strip sits flush with the card. */
.generation-row__stats {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-left: -4px;
    margin-right: -4px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
}

.generation-row__creatures { flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap; }

/* --surface-sunk, not --surface: a node now sits INSIDE a SectionCard whose
 * own fill is --surface, and white on white left the tiles readable only by
 * their 3px border. The token's own note is "inset rows, unselected
 * options", which is what a creature tile in a generation card is. */
.node {
    flex-direction: column;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-radius: var(--radius-tile);
    border-left-width: 3px;
    border-left-color: var(--divider);
}

.node.founder  { border-left-color: var(--amber); }
.node.mutated  { border-left-color: var(--violet); }

/* THE SECOND CHANNEL. bible 10.4 - "Colour never carries information alone."
 * The two rules above are 3px of colour on a tile that draws no creature, so
 * until Task 13 they were the WHOLE signal for Founder and Mutated. These
 * write the same facts in words, and `LineageView.MarksFor` builds the row
 * only when there is something in it - a node with nothing to say gets no
 * element, which is the eight-times-over lesson of this phase.
 *
 * ONE NEUTRAL INK FOR ALL THREE, and that is the whole point of the fix.
 * Drawing "Founder" in --amber and "Mutated" in --violet would put both
 * facts straight back onto the colour they were just taken off, and would
 * read as a tidier version of the same defect.
 *
 * --text-secondary, not --text-micro. bible 10.6's 11px floor is written
 * about numbers and these are words, so 10px would be legal - but a mark
 * that a player has to be able to read INSTEAD of the colour is not the
 * place to spend the last legible point.
 *
 * --ink, NOT --mute, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. These were
 * --mute for one capture. `Consumed` had been a plain Label - Theme.uss
 * gives those --ink at --text-body - so demoting it to a secondary grey made
 * the ONE fact this screen already stated in words HARDER to read than it
 * was before, and worst on exactly the tiles that need it: a consumed node
 * carries opacity 0.55, which multiplies whatever colour is set. Measured
 * off the captures, the darkest pixel of `Consumed` on a dimmed tile went
 * from 181 to 208 of 255. A mark that stands in for a colour cannot be
 * quieter than the colour was. This is the same size and ink the old state
 * line had, one point down so it sits under the name rather than beside it.
 *
 * NO COLOUR OF ITS OWN PER MARK, for the reason above: three words in one
 * ink, and the rail keeps the hue. */
.node__marks {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
}

.node__mark {
    font-size: var(--text-secondary);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
}

/* THE TRAIT STACK, AND THE RULE IS THE CONTAINER'S BECAUSE IT CANNOT BE THE
 * CHIP'S. `StatCell.uss`'s closing note has the measurement in full: a
 * component's stylesheet reaches the component and its descendants and never
 * its parent, so nothing `TraitChip` carries can space the column holding it.
 * `.splice-chamber-view__parent-traits` owns the identical pair of rules for
 * the identical reason, and this is deliberately the same two values so the
 * two tiles stack their chips the same way.
 *
 * A COLUMN RATHER THAN THE CHAMBER'S WRAPPING ROW, and the node's width is
 * why. A parent tile is 151px wide and fits two chips side by side; a node
 * sizes itself to its own content inside a wrapping row of siblings, and
 * measured on `LineageView.png` before this change it is 93-94px - narrower
 * than a single `Carapace III` chip (82px on `RosterView.png`, x=28..109).
 * A row here would wrap every node to two lines and widen the tile to no
 * purpose. Default `flex-direction` is already column, so this rule sets the
 * gap and nothing else.
 *
 * VERTICAL ONLY, AND THE HORIZONTAL GAP IS DELIBERATELY NOT RESTATED - the
 * chamber's note beside its own copy of this rule has the six pixels it cost
 * there. `TraitChip` already owns the handoff's `gap: 4px` as 2px of margin
 * on each of its own sides. */
.node__traits { margin-top: var(--space-1); }

.node__traits > .trait-chip { margin-bottom: var(--space-1); }

/* Dimmed, never removed: a consumed parent still being in the record is the
 * thing this screen exists to show. */
.node.consumed { opacity: 0.55; }

/* THE ONE NODE THE SCREEN IS ABOUT - bible 9.2's "a creature they named at
 * the bottom of it".
 *
 * IT USED TO SWALLOW ITS OWN TRAIT MARKS. `.trait-pip`'s fill is
 * --violet-tint (`TraitPip.uss:11`) and so is this rule's, so until Phase 9
 * Task 19 the highlighted node's two pills were drawn in exactly the colour
 * behind them and had no edge of any kind: measured on `LineageView.png`,
 * `Ash (G1)`'s tile reads a flat #f1ecfa from y=207 to y=354 while the same
 * pills on the tile beside it are clearly pills. No test could see it - a
 * class list cannot tell you that two classes resolve to one colour - and the
 * capture is what showed it. The marks are `TraitChip`s now and carry the
 * species tint, so the collision survives for Hollow alone, one species in
 * six rather than six in six. Moving THIS colour instead was not available:
 * nothing in the bundle draws this screen, so a new highlight fill would be
 * a value with no source. */
.node.highlight { background-color: var(--violet-tint); }
